course: everyday
lesson: 245
title: Crafts: scissors, glue, paint and needles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Making things is great fun, but craft tools can cut, prick, stain, stick and burn, so each one has a simple safe way to use it.
explain: Scissors cut away from your body and are carried with the points down; needles live in a pincushion and are counted back at the end.
explain: Glue sticks, white glue and markers dry out if left open, so the lid or cap always goes back on; super glue and kilns are for adults only.
explain: Paint stains clothes, so you wear an old shirt or apron, and you wash brushes straight away, before the paint dries hard.
explain: A girl in Toronto counts her sewing needles back into the pincushion: nine out, nine back, so her cat will not step on one.
fact: Children use child safety scissors, cut away from their body and walk with the points down.
fact: White glue and glue sticks are used a little at a time, the lid goes back on, and hands are washed after.
fact: Super glue sticks skin together in seconds and can even glue eyelids, so only an adult uses it.
fact: An old shirt or apron protects your clothes from paint, which stains.
fact: If paint splashes in your eye, rinse it with clean water and tell an adult.
fact: Paintbrushes are washed straight after painting, because dry paint goes hard and ruins them.
fact: Watercolour and acrylic paint rinse out in water; oil paint needs an adult and a special cleaner, which can be poisonous.
fact: Permanent markers do not wash off, so they are used only on paper or with permission, and the cap goes back on.
fact: Glitter is used over a tray and kept away from eyes, because it is hard to clean and hurts eyes.
fact: Clay is kept moist under plastic, hands are washed after, and the kiln (a very hot oven that bakes clay into pottery) is only for adults because it is extremely hot.
fact: Knitting and sewing needles are kept in a pincushion and counted back, so none are left to prick people or pets.
fact: Chalk drawings belong on your own driveway or a quiet path, never on the road where cars come.
exercise: How do you walk across the classroom holding scissors? | With the points down | With the points up in the air | Pointing them forward | Opening and closing them
exercise: Which way should you cut with scissors? | Away from your body | Towards your body | Towards your friend | Towards your other hand
exercise: You have finished with the glue stick. What do you do? | Put the lid back on and wash your hands | Leave it open on the table so it is ready for next time | Put it in your pocket open | Lick the sticky glue off your fingers to clean them
exercise: Your model plane is broken and needs super glue. Who should use it? | Only an adult | You, if you are careful | Any older child | Whoever is nearest
exercise: Why is super glue dangerous for children? | It sticks skin together in seconds | It smells of strawberries, so children try to eat it | It turns your skin blue for a week | It only works on paper
exercise: You are about to paint at the kitchen table. What should you wear? | An old shirt or an apron | Your best school jumper | Your new party dress | Nothing over your clothes
exercise: When should you wash your paintbrushes? | Straight after painting | The next morning | Once a week | Only when they look dirty
exercise: You want to colour a picture with a permanent marker. Where may you use it? | Only on paper or with permission | On the wall or the kitchen table | On your arm or hand, because it washes off at bedtime | On anything at all that you like
exercise: Why should glitter be used over a tray? | It is hard to clean and spreads everywhere | It sticks much better to things on a tray | It loses all its sparkle otherwise | Glitter must be kept cold, and a tray keeps it cool
exercise: Your clay figure is not finished at bedtime. How do you keep it? | Moist, under plastic | In the sun to stay soft | In the freezer | In a bowl of hot water
exercise: Who uses the kiln that bakes pottery? | Only adults | Any child with gloves | The fastest child | Whoever made the pot
exercise: You finish sewing. What do you do with the needles? | Put them in the pincushion and count them back | Leave them on the sofa cushion for next time | Drop them loose into the kitchen bin | Stick them in the carpet
exercise: You painted with oil paint. How are the brushes cleaned? | An adult uses a special cleaner | You rinse them in the sink with water | You leave them to dry overnight | You wash them in the bath
exercise: Where is the best place to draw with sidewalk chalk? | Your own driveway or a quiet path | The middle of the road | A busy car park | The bus lane
test: A boy in Leeds runs across the art room holding open scissors with the points up. What could happen? | He could trip and stab himself or someone else | The scissors would become blunt | Nothing, because child scissors can never hurt anyone at all | His teacher would give him a prize for being so quick
test: Why does a glue stick need its lid put back on? | Without the lid it dries out | The lid keeps the glue warm | The colour fades without the lid | It stops the glue from melting
test: A girl in Texas gets a drop of super glue between two fingers. What does this show? | Super glue sticks skin together very fast | Super glue is perfectly safe for children | Super glue washes off at once with cold water | Super glue only sticks to wood
test: Sam forgot to wash his acrylic paintbrush and left it until Saturday. What happened? | The paint dried hard and ruined the brush | The brush got softer and better to paint with | The paint turned into water | The brush grew longer bristles while it was waiting
test: Why should a cap go back on a permanent marker straight away? | It dries out if left open | It becomes washable if left open | It changes colour in the air | It starts to leak ink
test: Glitter flies up and lands near a girl's face. Why is this a problem? | Glitter can hurt the eyes | Glitter makes you sneeze colours | Glitter melts on skin | Glitter is poisonous to touch
test: A grandma in Dublin knits with her grandson. Why do they count the needles at the end? | So none are left out to prick people or pets | So they know how many more needles to buy | Because needles go missing at night | To check that none of the needles have grown longer
test: Kids want to draw a chalk hopscotch in the road in front of their house. What is wiser? | Draw it on the driveway or a quiet path | Draw it fast before cars come | Draw it in the middle of the road | Draw it near the corner
test: running to the table w the scissors to show sara! | Walk with the points down, never run | Run with the points up so u don't trip on them | Run fast so it's quick | Throw them over to her, it's quicker than running
test: dad's super glue is on the shelf, can i use it for my model | No, only an adult uses super glue | Yes, it's just like a glue stick | Yes, if you're careful with your eyes | Only a big drop of it
test: done painting, i'll wash the brushes tomorrow | Wash them now, dry paint ruins them | Tomorrow is fine, paint stays wet for days | Leave them standing in the paint pot | Put them in the drawer just as they are
test: can i do chalk drawings on the road out front, no cars rn | No, only on your driveway or a quiet path | Yes, if no cars are coming now | Yes, roads are the biggest space | Only in the middle line
explain: A boy in Texas ran across the classroom holding open scissors; he tripped and the blade went into his leg, and he needed stitches.
explain: A girl in Wales got super glue on her fingers and pulled them apart in a panic; she tore her skin and it was sore for a week.
fact: Running with scissors or a craft knife can turn a small trip into a deep stab wound.
fact: Skin stuck with super glue is soaked in warm soapy water and gently rolled apart, never pulled, and glue in the eye needs a doctor.
test: gonna run over to show my mate with the scissors | Walk, holding them closed with the points down | Run, it's only a few steps | Run with the points up | Throw them to him
test: Why must you not pull apart fingers stuck with super glue? | You can tear the skin | It makes the glue stronger | The glue spreads to your face | The fingers stay stuck forever anyway
test: paint splashed in my eye, it stings | Rinse it with clean water and tell an adult | Rub it hard with ur fist to get it out | Keep it shut and carry on painting | Wipe it with your painty sleeve
test: did u hear a kid glued his eyelids together with super glue doing crafts? real lesson? | Only an adult uses super glue | Use super glue but keep your eyes shut | Super glue washes off with water easily | Super glue only sticks to paper
test: apparently a girl tripped running with scissors and they went right into her leg. what's the true lesson? | Walk with scissors, points down, never run | Run with scissors closed and it's totally fine | Carry them with the points up instead | Only the big scissors are dangerous, not school ones
test: my mate says if u get super glue on ur fingers u have to rip them apart quick. right? | No, soak in warm soapy water and roll them apart | Yes, pull hard and fast | Yes, then put more glue on | Yes, use a knife to cut them
exam: Identify the person who may use super glue. | An adult only | Any child over six | Any child wearing gloves | Any child with permission from a friend
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises fingers stuck together with super glue? | They are soaked in warm soapy water and gently rolled apart | They are pulled apart forcefully at once | They are left until the glue has dissolved of its own accord overnight | They are separated with a sharp pair of scissors
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice for the carrying of scissors. | Child safety scissors are carried points down | Scissors are carried open and points up | Scissors are carried in the mouth | Scissors are thrown to the next person
exam: Ascertain the reason for which needles used in sewing are counted back into a pincushion. | So that none remain to prick people or pets | So that the teacher may count them at the end of each lesson | So that they remain sharp | So that they do not rust when left in a damp room
